DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     kkhheellpp provides a framework for managing kkhheellpp modules, which indirectly
     use the hhook(9) KPI to register their hook functions with hook points of
     interest within the kernel.  Khelp modules aim to provide a structured
     way to dynamically extend the kernel at runtime in an ABI preserving man‐
     ner.  Depending on the subsystem providing hook points, a kkhheellpp module
     may be able to associate per-object data for maintaining relevant state
     between hook calls.  The hhook(9) and kkhheellpp frameworks are tightly inte‐
     grated and anyone interested in kkhheellpp should also read the hhook(9) man‐
     ual page thoroughly.

   IInnffoorrmmaattiioonn ffoorr KKhheellpp MMoodduullee IImmpplleemmeennttoorrss
     kkhheellpp modules are represented within the kkhheellpp framework by a _s_t_r_u_c_t
     _h_e_l_p_e_r which has the following members:

           struct helper {
                   int (*mod_init) (void);
                   int (*mod_destroy) (void);
           #define HELPER_NAME_MAXLEN 16
                   char                    h_name[HELPER_NAME_MAXLEN];
                   uma_zone_t              h_zone;
                   struct hookinfo         *h_hooks;
                   uint32_t                h_nhooks;
                   uint32_t                h_classes;
                   int32_t                 h_id;
                   volatile uint32_t       h_refcount;
                   uint16_t                h_flags;
                   TAILQ_ENTRY(helper)     h_next;
           };

     Modules must instantiate a _s_t_r_u_c_t _h_e_l_p_e_r, but are only required to set
     the _h___c_l_a_s_s_e_s field, and may optionally set the _h___f_l_a_g_s, _m_o_d___i_n_i_t and
     _m_o_d___d_e_s_t_r_o_y fields where required.  The framework takes care of all other
     fields and modules should refrain from manipulating them.  Using the C99
     designated initialiser feature to set fields is encouraged.

     If specified, the _m_o_d___i_n_i_t function will be run by the kkhheellpp framework
     prior to completing the registration process.  Returning a non-zero value
     from the _m_o_d___i_n_i_t function will abort the registration process and fail
     to load the module.  If specified, the _m_o_d___d_e_s_t_r_o_y function will be run
     by the kkhheellpp framework during the deregistration process, after the mod‐
     ule has been deregistered by the kkhheellpp framework.  The return value is
     currently ignored.  Valid kkhheellpp classes are defined in <_s_y_s_/_k_h_e_l_p_._h>.
     Valid flags are defined in <_s_y_s_/_m_o_d_u_l_e___k_h_e_l_p_._h>.  The HELPER_NEEDS_OSD
     flag should be set in the _h___f_l_a_g_s field if the kkhheellpp module requires per‐
     sistent per-object data storage.  There is no programmatic way (yet) to
     check if a kkhheellpp class provides the ability for kkhheellpp modules to asso‐
     ciate persistent per-object data, so a manual check is required.

     The KKHHEELLPP__DDEECCLLAARREE__MMOODD() and KKHHEELLPP__DDEECCLLAARREE__MMOODD__UUMMAA() macros provide conve‐
     nient wrappers around the DECLARE_MODULE(9) macro, and are used to regis‐
     ter a kkhheellpp module with the kkhheellpp framework.  KKHHEELLPP__DDEECCLLAARREE__MMOODD__UUMMAA()
     should only be used by modules which require the use of persistent per-
     object storage i.e. modules which set the HELPER_NEEDS_OSD flag in their
     _s_t_r_u_c_t _h_e_l_p_e_r's _h___f_l_a_g_s field.

     The first four arguments common to both macros are as follows.  The _h_n_a_m_e
     argument specifies the unique ascii(7) name for the kkhheellpp module.  It
     should be no longer than HELPER_NAME_MAXLEN-1 characters in length.  The
     _h_d_a_t_a argument is a pointer to the module's _s_t_r_u_c_t _h_e_l_p_e_r.  The _h_h_o_o_k_s
     argument points to a static array of _s_t_r_u_c_t _h_o_o_k_i_n_f_o structures.  The ar‐
     ray should contain a _s_t_r_u_c_t _h_o_o_k_i_n_f_o for each hhook(9) point the module
     wishes to hook, even when using the same hook function multiple times for
     different hhook(9) points.  The _v_e_r_s_i_o_n argument specifies a version num‐
     ber for the module which will be passed to MODULE_VERSION(9).  The
     KKHHEELLPP__DDEECCLLAARREE__MMOODD__UUMMAA() macro takes the additional _c_t_o_r and _d_t_o_r argu‐
     ments, which specify optional uma(9) constructor and destructor func‐
     tions.  NULL should be passed where the functionality is not required.

     The kkhheellpp__ggeett__iidd() function returns the numeric identifier for the kkhheellpp
     module with name _h_n_a_m_e.

     The kkhheellpp__ggeett__oossdd() function is used to obtain the per-object data
     pointer for a specified kkhheellpp module.  The _h_o_s_d argument is a pointer to
     the underlying subsystem object's _s_t_r_u_c_t _o_s_d.  This is provided by the
     hhook(9) framework when calling into a kkhheellpp module's hook function.  The
     _i_d argument specifies the numeric identifier for the kkhheellpp module to ex‐
     tract the data pointer from _h_o_s_d for.  The _i_d is obtained using the
     kkhheellpp__ggeett__iidd() function.

     The kkhheellpp__aadddd__hhhhooookk() and kkhheellpp__rreemmoovvee__hhhhooookk() functions allow a kkhheellpp
     module to dynamically hook/unhook hhook(9) points at run time.  The _h_k_i
     argument specifies a pointer to a _s_t_r_u_c_t _h_o_o_k_i_n_f_o which encapsulates the
     required information about the hhook(9) point and hook function being ma‐
     nipulated.  The HHOOK_WAITOK flag may be passed in via the _f_l_a_g_s argument
     of kkhheellpp__aadddd__hhhhooookk() if malloc(9) is allowed to sleep waiting for memory
     to become available.

   IInntteeggrraattiinngg KKhheellpp IInnttoo aa KKeerrnneell SSuubbssyysstteemm
     Most of the work required to allow kkhheellpp modules to do useful things re‐
     lates to defining and instantiating suitable hhook(9) points for kkhheellpp
     modules to hook into.  The only additional decision a subsystem needs to
     make is whether it wants to allow kkhheellpp modules to associate persistent
     per-object data.  Providing support for persistent data storage can allow
     kkhheellpp modules to perform more complex functionality which may be desir‐
     able.  Subsystems which want to allow Khelp modules to associate persis‐
     tent per-object data with one of the subsystem's data structures need to
     make the following two key changes:

     ••   Embed a _s_t_r_u_c_t _o_s_d pointer in the structure definition for the ob‐
         ject.

     ••   Add calls to kkhheellpp__iinniitt__oossdd() and kkhheellpp__ddeessttrrooyy__oossdd() to the subsys‐
         tem code paths which are responsible for respectively initialising
         and destroying the object.

     The kkhheellpp__iinniitt__oossdd() function initialises the per-object data storage for
     all currently loaded kkhheellpp modules of appropriate classes which have set
     the HELPER_NEEDS_OSD flag in their _h___f_l_a_g_s field.  The _c_l_a_s_s_e_s argument
     specifies a bitmask of kkhheellpp classes which this subsystem associates
     with.  If a kkhheellpp module matches any of the classes in the bitmask, that
     module will be associated with the object.  The _h_o_s_d argument specifies
     the pointer to the object's _s_t_r_u_c_t _o_s_d which will be used to provide the
     persistent storage for use by kkhheellpp modules.

     The kkhheellpp__ddeessttrrooyy__oossdd() function frees all memory that was associated
     with an object's _s_t_r_u_c_t _o_s_d by a previous call to kkhheellpp__iinniitt__oossdd().  The
     _h_o_s_d argument specifies the pointer to the object's _s_t_r_u_c_t _o_s_d which will
     be purged in preparation for destruction.

IIMMPPLLEEMMEENNTTAATTIIOONN NNOOTTEESS
     kkhheellpp modules are protected from being prematurely unloaded by a refer‐
     ence count.  The count is incremented each time a subsystem calls
     kkhheellpp__iinniitt__oossdd() causing persistent storage to be allocated for the mod‐
     ule, and decremented for each corresponding call to kkhheellpp__ddeessttrrooyy__oossdd().
     Only when a module's reference count has dropped to zero can the module
     be unloaded.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     The kkhheellpp__iinniitt__oossdd() function returns zero if no errors occurred.  It re‐
     turns ENOMEM if a kkhheellpp module which requires per-object storage fails to
     allocate the necessary memory.

     The kkhheellpp__ddeessttrrooyy__oossdd() function only returns zero to indicate that no
     errors occurred.

     The kkhheellpp__ggeett__iidd() function returns the unique numeric identifier for the
     registered kkhheellpp module with name _h_n_a_m_e.  It return -1 if no module with
     the specified name is currently registered.

     The kkhheellpp__ggeett__oossdd() function returns the pointer to the kkhheellpp module's
     persistent object storage memory.  If the module identified by _i_d does
     not have persistent object storage registered with the object's _h_o_s_d
     _s_t_r_u_c_t _o_s_d, NULL is returned.

     The kkhheellpp__aadddd__hhhhooookk() function returns zero if no errors occurred.  It
     returns ENOENT if it could not find the requested hhook(9) point.  It re‐
     turns ENOMEM if malloc(9) failed to allocate memory.  It returns EEXIST
     if attempting to register the same hook function more than once for the
     same hhook(9) point.

     The kkhheellpp__rreemmoovvee__hhhhooookk() function returns zero if no errors occurred.  It
     returns ENOENT if it could not find the requested hhook(9) point.

